  "articleBody": "Thinking about insulating your garage? That’s a smart move for comfort and energy efficiency, but there are a few things you should know before jumping in. First, you'll want to assess your garage's current state. This means checking your framing cavities, existing sheathing, and looking for any moisture issues. You'd be surprised how often we find hidden dampness that needs addressing before insulation can even begin. Next, consider what kind of insulation makes the most sense for your space. There are options like fiberglass batts, cellulose, spray foam, and rigid foam, each with its own R-value and benefits. The best choice depends on your local climate, your budget, and whether you plan to convert the garage into living space. For conversions, you'll definitely need to meet specific energy codes. After framing and any rough electrical or plumbing work is done, that's the sweet spot for insulation installation – before any drywall or interior finishes go up. If you're using batts, make sure they're cut to fit snugly without compression, and leave space around electrical boxes. Finally, air sealing is huge! Don't just focus on the insulation; properly sealing around penetrations and rim joists prevents drafts and moisture problems. If you're in the Denver area, HH Remodeling LLC can help you navigate these choices and ensure your garage insulation is installed correctly for lasting comfort and efficiency.",
